A||N||9||Father-of-Fiero-owner||auto-trans problem||||1||
Z||000000||Father-of-Fiero-owner||12-12-1999||09:43 PM||||My sons 84 SE 2.5L w/auto trans started to slip in drive the other night. Low,second,and reverse work fine. I checked the fluid and it's full. Any help would be appreciated. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||12-12-1999||10:09 PM||||If the fluid is full there is more than likely something broke or worn out.  Before you start taking stuff apart tho, you might try changing the filter and putting new fluid in.  If this hasn't been done in a while, it could keep the fluid from getting every place it needs to.  Remote possiblilty, but worth a shot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||kornholio||12-12-1999||10:16 PM||||I agree with Sage.  Change the tranny fluid and filter first.  My reverse stopped working and the mechanic told me that the tranny fluid was dirty as $#!+.  Once it was changed, everything was fine again.  I thought I really messed up the transmission because it stopped working right after I did a neutral drop...never doing that again!||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||12-13-1999||02:55 PM||||Also, if you have one of those little drill operated pumps, you should pump your tranny completely dry and refill it if your going to change the fluid.  Just dropping the pan and refilling it does not empty the Torque converter.<BR>Speaking of the Torque Converter, you also might have a problem with the Torque converter clutch Solenoid.  you can test this theory by disconnecting the one 4 prong connector from your tranny and taking it for a spin.  <P>If this fixes your problem, you need to replace the TCC Lockup solenoid, Dont just leave the wire disconnected.  Leaving the wire disconnected is a short term solution that will kill the tranny.  Replacing that solenoid is not that big of a deal.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||12-13-1999||05:21 PM||||If I'm not mistaken pumping out the transmission still won't get the fluid out of the torque converter.  You can flush the entire system with new fluid but I don't think you can completely empty it that way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-13-1999||08:38 PM||||draw a vacuum on the bottom tranny cooler line. this should pull a good bit out of the TC and the rest of the tranny guts. You can do this with a drill driven pump. you want the tranny oil at around 90-100 dgrees F.<P>disconnect both cooler lines and blow them out to get a bit more. Don't fool with the cooler up front. You can make the radiator leak if you aren't carefull with that.<P>Those two items should get you up around 4 quarts in a change.<P>There is a way to get as much as 5.5-6 quarts out, but I don't describe it because if it's not done carefully you can damage the tranny or kill someone.<P>((Total ATF volume is around 6.5-7 quarts as I recall.))<P>Use the new Dexron 3 when filling.<P>The TC lock solinoid is under the side cover. replace it at the same time, even if it's not bad. it's cheap insurance.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Father-of-Fiero-owner||12-13-1999||08:45 PM||||Thanks for all the info. The first day off I have I will give it a try. My son is 16 yrs. old and is getting anxious to get it up and going. He turns 17 on Thurs. and bought this with his own cash so hopefully by then.<p>[This message has been edited by Father-of-Fiero-owner (edited 12-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||12-14-1999||02:53 AM||||I read someplace that, unless you change the fluid every 30,000 miles, you should never try to replace fluid other than what it takes to change the filter.  If you do have to change the fluid you should take it to a trans shop where they can completely flush and refill it.  Is this true?||||reg||5
Z||000008||theogre||12-14-1999||06:57 PM||||only if the old fluid is burnt. Then you likely have other problems any way.<P>As I said above. it's posible to pump down the tranny quite a bit. It won't drain. It has to be pumped from the outlet port to the cooler. ((I got a bit over 6 quarts out of mine. Without "flushing"))<P>BTW. There should be a flat donut magnet in the pan. wipe it clean and put it back. If it's missing replace it. it doesn't have to be an exact replacement. A couple refrigerator magnets that stick on both sides will work. (((Some flat magnets only pull strong on one face. These are no good here.)))||||reg||1
Z||000009||dogtired||12-15-1999||03:27 PM||||If you end up taking it to a tranny shop, ASK QUESTIONS first. There was one shop here in Kansas that would drill a hole in torque converters to drain them, then put a plug in it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>They said that was the only way to drain a GM converter. I will never take my car there. Just a word of caution!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
